//
// loft#1457 — a SHORT key (fewer subscripts than the declaration names) at every keyed kind.
//
// Three kinds refused it and `spatial` did not: `s[5]` on a `spatial<P[x, y]>` built a complete
// lookup out of a malformed key and answered null, with no diagnostic — and null is also what a
// genuine miss answers, so the two were indistinguishable to the author.
//
// **The rules settle what the issue filed as a design question.**  There is a reading under
// which `s[5]` is a raw MORTON-CODE lookup, since a spatial's stored form is a Morton interval
// and one integer is a well-formed code.  `formal/collections.md` does not admit it: `(Col-Trie)`
// shows what it looks like when a kind's own operation IS admitted — *"a PREFIX slice — the
// operation the kind exists for"* — while `(Col-Spatial)` names 1–3 coordinate AXES and an
// internal representation (*"Morton/Z-order radix tree … the runtime Parts variant is `Radix`"*)
// and no surface operation beyond the axes.  An implementation detail is not a surface, so the
// short key is refused.
//
// `index` and `sorted` are the exception and keep their own message: a partial key there is a
// REAL operation, rewritten to `idx[k1..=k1]` and iterated, refused only in a value position.
// `spatial` is not that case even though a Morton code has prefixes — a Z-order prefix is a
// QUADRANT, not a 1-D range, so routing it through the range path would iterate the wrong
// records instead of reporting.  That is why the fix refuses rather than widening the iterating
// set, and why the predicate is stated as the COMPLEMENT of the kinds that iterate: a kind
// added to the language refuses by default, and the cost of an omission here is a silent null.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  the keyed KIND (hash · spatial · index · sorted), which is the whole
//                        point — the defect was one kind missing from a hand-named set.
// ⚠ The two expectations name their COLLECTION, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy.
// Both cells first expected the bare *"Too few key fields"* — the message as it stood — and
// `falsify.sh` reported the file INERT: two cells expecting one identical string are satisfied
// by ONE error between them, so the guard passed on the build it was written to catch.  The
// message now names the type and both counts, which makes the cells distinguishable and is a
// better diagnostic besides (it says how many are wanted, and on what).  `1423-…` states the
// rule this file re-learned: each cell names what the author wrote, which is what keeps the
// cells from making each other vacuous.
//
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the key is SHORT by exactly one, and the declaration has two fields.
//                        The legitimate spellings and the index/sorted controls are `1457b`'s,
//                        because a firing `@EXPECT_ERROR` stops the run and can assert nothing
//                        after it.
//
// @falsified-at: 61bfbd96 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 2/2, native
//   exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 2/2.  Re-measured after the message gained its
//   type: the FIRST draft, with both cells expecting the bare "Too few key fields", read INERT
//   on all four rows — the guard passed on the build it was written to catch.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees. Falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct P1457 { x: integer, y: integer, v: integer }
struct H1457 { a: integer, b: integer, v: integer }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Too few key fields for `spatial<P1457[x, y]>` — 1 given, 2 declared
fn spatial_short() -> integer {
  s: spatial<P1457[x, y]> = [P1457 { x: 1, y: 2, v: 7 }];
  s[5].v ?? -1
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Too few key fields for `hash<H1457[a, b]>` — 1 given, 2 declared
fn hash_short() -> integer {
  h: hash<H1457[a, b]> = [H1457 { a: 1, b: 2, v: 7 }];
  h[5].v ?? -1
}

// `index` and `sorted` are NOT here even though they refuse too, and the reason is mechanical:
// the parse stops after these two reports, so a third and fourth expectation in this file are
// never reached and read as "expected error never emitted".  Their control lives in `1457b`
// and is stronger for being runnable — that a partial key still ITERATES on a `sorted` says
// the fix did not flatten them into this refusal, where re-asserting their message would only
// say the message still exists.
